  "question": {
    "text": "In a sample initially, there are an equal number of atoms of two radioactive isotopes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math> days later, the number of atoms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> is twice that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>. The half-life of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>1.5</mtext></math> days. What is the half-life (in days) of isotope <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>?",
    "image": null
  },
